.TH "CSVSTACK" "1" "Aug 16, 2024" "2.2.0" "csvkit"
.SH NAME
csvstack \- csvstack Documentation
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
Stack up the rows from multiple CSV files, optionally adding a grouping value to each row:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
usage: csvstack [\-h] [\-d DELIMITER] [\-t] [\-q QUOTECHAR] [\-u {0,1,2,3}] [\-b]
                [\-p ESCAPECHAR] [\-z FIELD_SIZE_LIMIT] [\-e ENCODING] [\-S] [\-H]
                [\-K SKIP_LINES] [\-v] [\-l] [\-\-zero] [\-V] [\-g GROUPS]
                [\-n GROUP_NAME] [\-\-filenames] [\-\-dialect FILE=DIALECT]
                [\-\-dialect\-file FILE]
                [FILE [FILE ...]]

Stack up the rows from multiple CSV files, optionally adding a grouping value.

positional arguments:
  FILE                  The CSV file(s) to operate on. If omitted, will accept
                        input as piped data via STDIN.

optional arguments:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-g GROUPS, \-\-groups GROUPS
                        A comma\-separated list of values to add as \(dqgrouping
                        factors\(dq, one per CSV being stacked. These are added
                        to the output as a new column. You may specify a name
                        for the new column using the \-n flag.
  \-n GROUP_NAME, \-\-group\-name GROUP_NAME
                        A name for the grouping column, e.g. \(dqyear\(dq. Only used
                        when also specifying \-g.
  \-\-filenames           Use the filename of each input file as its grouping
                        value. When specified, \-g will be ignored.
  \-\-dialect FILE=DIALECT, \-\-dialects FILE=DIALECT
                        Specify the dialect of one input file, as
                        FILE=DIALECT. Repeat for each input. The dialect can
                        be a name (semicolon, tab, comma, pipe), a single
                        delimiter, or key=value options (delimiter, quotechar,
                        quoting, doublequote, escapechar, skipinitialspace,
                        strict). A comma\-separated list of dialects is
                        applied to the inputs in order. Inputs not specified
                        use the common input options. Output always uses
                        csvkit\(aqs default format.
  \-\-dialect\-file FILE, \-\-dialects\-file FILE
                        Read FILE=DIALECT declarations from a file. Each line
                        can declare one input, or a JSON object can map
                        filenames to dialects. Can be specified multiple
                        times.
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
See also: \fI\%Arguments common to all tools\fP\&.
.sp
\fBWARNING:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If you redirect output to an input file like \fBcsvstack file.csv > file.csv\fP, the file will grow indefinitely.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH INPUT DIALECTS
.sp
By default, every input is read with the common \fI\%Arguments common to all tools\fP\&. If inputs use different delimiters or
quoting conventions, declare each input\(aqs dialect separately:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
csvstack \-\-dialect daily\-01.csv=semicolon \-\-dialect daily\-02.tsv=tab daily\-01.csv daily\-02.tsv
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The declaration is \fBFILE=DIALECT\fP\&. \fBFILE\fP can be a path, a basename, \fB\-\fP for standard input, or a 1\-based input
position. \fBDIALECT\fP can be a name (\fBcomma\fP, \fBsemicolon\fP, \fBtab\fP, \fBpipe\fP), a single delimiter, or a
space\-separated list of \fBkey=value\fP options: \fBdelimiter\fP, \fBquotechar\fP, \fBquoting\fP, \fBdoublequote\fP,
\fBescapechar\fP, \fBskipinitialspace\fP and \fBstrict\fP\&. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
csvstack \-\-dialect \(dqdaily\-01.csv=delimiter=; quotechar=\(aq\e\e\(dq\(aq\(dq daily\-01.csv
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A comma\-separated list of dialects is applied to the inputs in order:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
csvstack \-\-dialect \(dqsemicolon,tab\(dq daily\-01.csv daily\-02.tsv
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Inputs not listed in a declaration are read with the common input options, as before. The output is always written
with csvkit\(aqs default format (comma\-delimited, double\-quoted, Unix line endings), regardless of the input dialects.
.sp
Declarations can also be kept in a file, one \fBFILE=DIALECT\fP per line, with blank lines and \fB#\fP comments ignored:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
# daily reports from the same batch
daily\-01.csv=semicolon
daily\-02.tsv=tab
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
csvstack \-\-dialect\-file daily\-dialects.txt daily\-01.csv daily\-02.tsv
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The file can instead be a JSON object that maps filenames to dialects. A header row of \fBfile,dialect\fP is also
accepted.
.sp
If an input does not match its declared dialect, \fBcsvstack\fP exits with status 1 and reports the input filename
and the source line at which the bad record started, for example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
daily\-01.csv: line 3: unexpected end of data
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH EXAMPLES
.sp
Join a set of files for different years:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
csvstack \-g 2009,2010 examples/realdata/FY09_EDU_Recipients_by_State.csv examples/realdata/Datagov_FY10_EDU_recp_by_State.csv
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Add a single column to the left of a CSV:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
csvstack \-n NEWCOL \-g \(dq\(dq examples/dummy.csv
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
